RoboDR is dual-licensed, mirroring the Pulsar base it builds on:

  - RoboDR userspace code (every Rust crate in this workspace) is licensed
    under the Apache License 2.0. SPDX: Apache-2.0. This is the `license`
    value in Cargo.toml, so cargo, cargo-deny and the SBOM all see a valid
    SPDX expression.
  - Any eBPF probe code (`*.bpf.c` and generated skeletons) is licensed under
    GPL-2.0 (SPDX: GPL-2.0-only), as required for programs that load into the
    Linux kernel and use GPL-only BPF helpers. Each probe source carries its
    own `SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only` header. Probes are separate
    programs loaded into the kernel; they are not linked into the Apache-2.0
    userspace code as Rust code.

Pulsar describes the same split with the free-text string "Apache-2.0 WITH BPF
probes exception under GPL-2.0", which is not a valid SPDX expression; RoboDR
uses "Apache-2.0" in Cargo metadata and documents the probe licensing here.
GPL-licensed *userspace* dependencies are rejected by CI (see deny.toml).

See LICENSES/LICENSE-APACHE-2.0 and LICENSES/LICENSE-GPL-2.0 for the full texts.

Pulsar (vendored under vendor/pulsar) is Copyright Exein and licensed under the
same Apache-2.0 / GPL-2.0 split; see vendor/pulsar/LICENSE.
